<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">4623315</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2138983</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>REQUIREMENT OF THYMUS (T) LYMPHOCYTES FOR RESISTANCE TO LISTERIOSIS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Lane</surname><given-names>F. C.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Unanue</surname><given-names>E. R.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x32fae60N0x1daf8f8">From the Department of Pathology, Harvard Medical School, Boston, Massachusetts 02115</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>5</month><year>1972</year></pub-date><volume>135</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>1104</fpage><lpage>1112</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>23</day><month>1</month><year>1972</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; 1972 by The Rockefeller University Press</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> Spleen cells of mice infected with <italic>Listeria monocytogenes</italic> were adoptively transferred to normal mice. Such lymphocytes conferred resistance to a lethal challenge with <italic>Listeria</italic>. Hyperimmunization of the donor reduces the number of cells necessary to transfer effective immunity. Such spleen cells if treated with anti-&#x003b8; serum do not transfer resistance to <italic>Listeria</italic>. Hence, thymus (T) lymphocytes are involved in the resistance to infection with the facultative intracellular bacteria <italic>L. monocytogenes</italic>. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>